[{"_1":2},"llms/mdx",{"_3":4},"data","# Go PQC toolchain (/docs/pr-preview/pr-58/platform/pqc/go-toolchain)\n\n\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Go 1.27 includes ML-DSA in `crypto/mldsa` and in X.509 and TLS, but it has no composite or SLH-DSA certificates, no related certificates and no composite TLS signature schemes. Lamassu therefore builds its backend and its TLS proxy with a fork of Go, maintained on the `release-branch.go1.27` branch. This page describes what the fork adds, for developers who build Lamassu or write a client that must talk to it.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  <Callout type=\"warn\" title=\"Not standard Go\">\n    The fork exposes APIs that do not exist in the official Go distribution. Code that uses them compiles only with this toolchain, and a binary built with it must not be assumed to follow the Go compatibility promise for those APIs.\n  </Callout>\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  What the fork adds [#what-the-fork-adds]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  **Composite ML-DSA in X.509.** The `x509` package gains `CompositePublicKey` and `CompositePrivateKey`, which implement `crypto.Signer`, and 15 `CompositeAlgorithm` values (for example `x509.MLDSA65_ECDSA_P384_SHA512`) that can generate keys, sign and verify. Certificates, certificate requests and revocation lists signed with these keys parse, marshal and verify like any other. PKCS#8 encoding of composite private keys is supported. The [algorithms page](/docs/platform/pqc/algorithms) lists the identifiers.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  **SLH-DSA in X.509.** The `x509.SLHDSA` public key algorithm and twelve `PureSLHDSA...` signature algorithms are available for parsing and verifying certificates and keys. The implementation is the vendored `circl` library of Cloudflare. It is the least tested part of the fork: it has no x509 tests of its own and no TLS support.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  **Related certificates (RFC 9763).** The fork provides `x509.CreateBoundCertificate`, which adds the `id-pe-relatedCert` extension (`1.3.6.1.5.5.7.1.36`) to a certificate, `x509.VerifyRelatedCertificate`, and the helpers to build and check the `relatedCertRequest` attribute of a certificate request (`1.2.840.113549.1.9.16.2.60`). `x509.CertificateRequest` gains an `ExtraAttributes` field for carrying it. [Related certificates](/docs/platform/pqc/related-certificates) explains how Lamassu uses them.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  **Composite signature schemes in TLS.** The `tls` package adds 15 `SignatureScheme` constants, one per composite algorithm, advertised by default after the native ML-DSA schemes. They work only with TLS 1.3 and are tested with mutual authentication. See below for their code points.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  TLS code points [#tls-code-points]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  IANA has assigned no code points to the composite TLS signature schemes, so the fork uses the private-use range `0xFE00` to `0xFE0E`, in the order of the composite table: `0xFE00` is `MLDSA44-RSA2048-PSS-SHA256` and `0xFE0E` is `MLDSA65-Ed25519-SHA512`. A client or server built with a standard TLS library does not recognize them and will fall back to another scheme or fail the handshake. Interoperability is therefore limited to peers built from this fork, or to the native ML-DSA schemes (`0x0904` to `0x0906`) that standard Go 1.27 implements.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  The three variants that use RSA PKCS#1 v1.5 (`MLDSA44-RSA2048-PKCS15-SHA256`, `MLDSA65-RSA3072-PKCS15-SHA512` and `MLDSA65-RSA4096-PKCS15-SHA512`) are announced only in the `signature_algorithms_cert` extension, so they can appear in a certificate chain but a leaf certificate with such a key cannot sign the handshake.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  The key exchange is unchanged from upstream Go. Hybrid groups such as `X25519MLKEM768`, `SecP256r1MLKEM768`, `SecP384r1MLKEM1024` and the pure `MLKEM1024` are those of the Go 1.27 standard library.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  A minimal example [#a-minimal-example]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  The following program creates a self-signed composite certificate and uses it as the server certificate of a TLS 1.3 configuration. Error handling is omitted.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  ```go\n  alg := x509.MLDSA65_ECDSA_P384_SHA512\n  pub, priv, _ := alg.GenerateCompositeKey(rand.Reader)\n\n  tmpl := &x509.Certificate{\n      SerialNumber:          big.NewInt(1),\n      Subject:               pkix.Name{CommonName: \"example.com\"},\n      NotBefore:             time.Now().Add(-time.Hour),\n      NotAfter:              time.Now().Add(24 * time.Hour),\n      KeyUsage:              x509.KeyUsageDigitalSignature,\n      BasicConstraintsValid: true,\n      DNSNames:              []string{\"example.com\"},\n  }\n  der, _ := x509.CreateCertificate(rand.Reader, tmpl, tmpl, pub, priv)\n  keyDER, _ := x509.MarshalPKCS8PrivateKey(priv)\n\n  cert, _ := tls.X509KeyPair(\n      pem.EncodeToMemory(&pem.Block{Type: \"CERTIFICATE\", Bytes: der}),\n      pem.EncodeToMemory(&pem.Block{Type: \"PRIVATE KEY\", Bytes: keyDER}),\n  )\n  cfg := &tls.Config{\n      Certificates: []tls.Certificate{cert},\n      MinVersion:   tls.VersionTLS13,\n  }\n  ```\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  A composite key can also sign a message directly, with an optional context of at most 255 bytes:\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  ```go\n  sig, _ := priv.Sign(rand.Reader, msg, x509.CompositeSignerOpts{Context: ctx})\n  ok := alg.CompositeVerify(pub, msg, ctx, sig)\n  ```\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  What was removed [#what-was-removed]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Earlier iterations of the fork explored Chameleon certificates (`draft-bonnell-lamps-chameleon-certs`) and a delta-certificate construction. They were removed because they are no longer pursued. The related-certificate mechanism was later restored on its own, following RFC 9763.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Building the toolchain and the images [#building-the-toolchain-and-the-images]\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  The fork is built in CI by a workflow that runs the Go test suite and then publishes a multi-architecture image, `ghcr.io/lamassuiot/golang-pqc`, tagged `go1.27` and, for the `release-branch.go1.27` branch, `latest`. The image contains the toolchain under `/usr/local/go-pqc` with `GOROOT` pointing at it.\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  Lamassu consumes the toolchain in two ways. The backend images (CA, KMS, VA, alerts, device manager, DMS manager and the monolithic build) use `ghcr.io/lamassuiot/golang-pqc:latest` as their builder image. The backend CI workflow installs the same toolchain through a custom setup action in place of `actions/setup-go`, and the backend requires `go 1.27.3` in its module file. Traefik is not built from the image: its `build.sh` script expects a compiled checkout of the fork in a sibling directory and produces the binary outside a container. See [TLS front proxy with Traefik](/docs/platform/pqc/tls-front-proxy).\n</div>\n\n<div className=\"lm-diff-ins lm-diff-block\">\n  <Cards>\n    <Card title=\"Algorithms\" description=\"The identifiers and parameter sets behind these constants.\" href=\"/docs/platform/pqc/algorithms\" />\n\n    <Card title=\"TLS front proxy with Traefik\" description=\"How the fork is used to terminate device TLS.\" href=\"/docs/platform/pqc/tls-front-proxy\" />\n  </Cards>\n</div>\n"]
